I suggest the following...

When you find metals in the dungeon, they are almost always in crude form.
 Orcs and goblins and such use crude nuggets of gold for money.  In order
to purify metals, you must find a jeweler.  (New kind of shop.)  The
jeweler will assay and purify your metals for you, and give you coins. 
Jewelers also buy/sell/ID gems.  (ID costs 10% of gem's value.)

There should also be chunks of metal ores randomly scattered throughout
the "non-room" areas of each level, so an enterprising fellow with a
pick-axe (not a wand of digging!) should be able to make $$$...

As for coin conversion...

10 lead coins = 1 steel coin.
5 steel coins = 1 copper coin.
1 copper coin = 1 zorkmid.
5 copper coins = 1 silver coin.
10 silver coins = 1 gold coin.

The reason for lead and steel coins is to make trash treasure that
monsters carry.  They are near-worthless, and make for a lot of
encumbrance.  The reason for expensive coins (silver, gold) is to make
money easier to carry.  Jewelers will do exchanges for 1% of the amount
exchanged; other shopkeepers are more expensive.

In addition, I suggest the addition of a "wandering merchant".  The
merchant goes about, picking up items (also starts with several) and the
player can buy from him/her by #chatting.  Naturally, monsters will also
buy from the merchant.  "You hear a handshake in the distance..."
indicates a sale has been made...watch out.

